data.content:
Here's the approach — an **ambient context** via `Clock.UtcNow`:

**Production code** — just replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` → `Clock.UtcNow`:

```csharp
public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
    => Clock.UtcNow > dueDate;
```

**Tests** — pin the time with a disposable scope:

```csharp
using (Clock.Override(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)))
{
    Assert.True(calculator.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
}
```

**Why this is safe for parallel tests:** `AsyncLocal<T>` gives each async flow its own value, so `[Parallelizable]` / xUnit parallel collections won't conflict.

**Trade-offs:** One null-check per call (negligible). No DI plumbing needed. If you later add DI, you can migrate to `TimeProvider` (built-in on .NET 8+) at that point.
